item.text:
Exposure classification: `limited_exposure_with_choices`. Marcos had civilian nuclear-energy choices in 2022, but no evidence of nuclear-weapon command, custody, or crisis use authority. The chapter therefore stays at a null/non-scored research state.

No previously accepted web evidence to reuse.

Accepted source-claim units:
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"1987 Philippine Constitution","publisher":"LawPhil Project","publication_date":"1987","url":"https://lawphil.net/consti/cons1987.html","claim":"Article II states that the Philippines pursues a policy of freedom from nuclear weapons in its territory and an independent foreign policy.","locator":"Article II, Sections 7-8","source_type":"legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary constitutional text; static legal baseline.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Standing inherited constraint in 2022; not Marcos-specific.","ruler_attribution":"Inherited constitutional limit on Marcos's authority, not an action by Marcos.","contrary_evidence":["This establishes the baseline only; it does not show any 2022 ruler choice."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.5","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Treaty on the Non-Proliferation of Nuclear Weapons","publisher":"Supreme Court E-Library","publication_date":"1968-07-01","url":"https://elibrary.judiciary.gov.ph/thebookshelf/showdocs/35/10499","claim":"The Philippines became party to the NPT in 1972, and the treaty bars non-nuclear states from acquiring nuclear weapons while requiring safeguards for peaceful nuclear activity.","locator":"Preamble and Articles II-III; ratification note","source_type":"legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary treaty text with Philippine ratification note.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Standing treaty constraint in 2022; long-run baseline.","ruler_attribution":"Inherited treaty obligations, not a Marcos initiative in 2022.","contrary_evidence":["This is state-level constraint, not evidence of ruler-specific restraint or choice in 2022."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.5","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Treaty on the Southeast Asia Nuclear Weapon-Free Zone","publisher":"Supreme Court E-Library","publication_date":"1995-12-15","url":"https://elibrary.judiciary.gov.ph/thebookshelf/showdocs/36/12602","claim":"SEANWFZ bars states parties from developing, possessing, stationing, transporting, testing, or using nuclear weapons, including within their territory.","locator":"Article 3, Basic Undertakings","source_type":"legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary treaty text binding the Philippines and its ASEAN partners.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Standing regional obligation in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Inherited regional treaty constraint, not Marcos-specific conduct.","contrary_evidence":["This does not show any 2022 action by Marcos; it only defines the legal environment."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.5","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Country List: Philippines","publisher":"IAEA Office of Legal Affairs","publication_date":"2026-03-23","url":"https://ola.iaea.org/Applications/FactSheets/Country/Detail?code=PH","claim":"The Philippines has an in-force NPT safeguards agreement and an additional protocol with the IAEA.","locator":"Safeguards Agreements table, rows 1115 and 1851","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official IAEA country profile with treaty status.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Standing safeguards status in 2022; page updated later but the agreements predate 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Inherited safeguards framework, not a 2022 Marcos decision.","contrary_evidence":["The page shows peaceful-nuclear oversight, not nuclear-weapon control or crisis authority."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.9","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The Philippines ratifies the Treaty on the Prohibition of Nuclear Weapons (TPNW)","publisher":"United Nations Office for Disarmament Affairs","publication_date":"2021-02-22","url":"https://disarmament.unoda.org/en/spotlights/philippines-ratifies-treaty-prohibition-nuclear-weapons-tpnw","claim":"The Philippines ratified the TPNW in February 2021, before Marcos took office.","locator":"headline and date line","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official UN disarmament page documenting treaty ratification date.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Inherited baseline entering 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Pre-Marcos state act; useful only as inherited baseline.","contrary_evidence":["This predates Marcos and cannot be credited to his 2022 conduct."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"10th Review Conference of the Nuclear Non-Proliferation Treaty (NPT) High-Level Segment | Philippines","publisher":"Permanent Mission of the Republic of the Philippines to the United Nations","publication_date":"2022-08-01","url":"https://www.un.int/philippines/statements_speeches/10th-review-conference-nuclear-non-proliferation-treaty-npt-high-level-segment","claim":"The Philippine delegation said its NPT commitment stems from the aspiration to be free from nuclear weapons and from a path to sustainable development that includes peaceful nuclear energy.","locator":"lines 45-47","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official mission statement at a dated UN conference with clear attribution.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within Marcos's first months in office in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Philippine delegation under the Marcos administration; not an operational nuclear decision by Marcos personally.","contrary_evidence":["This is policy rhetoric, not evidence of nuclear-command authority or a material risk-bearing choice."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.4","1B.9","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"10th Review Conference of the Non-Proliferation Treaty (NPT) Main Committee I: Nuclear Disarmament | Philippines","publisher":"Permanent Mission of the Republic of the Philippines to the United Nations","publication_date":"2022-08-05","url":"https://www.un.int/philippines/statements_speeches/10th-review-conference-non-proliferation-treaty-npt-main-committee-i-nuclear","claim":"The Philippine delegation said nuclear weapons must never be used to resolve disputes and expressed concern about modernization, testing, and lack of progress in disarmament.","locator":"lines 48-52","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official mission statement with dated conference record.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and under Marcos's administration.","ruler_attribution":"State position attributable to the Marcos administration through its UN delegation, not to weapon control powers.","contrary_evidence":["This is a declaratory stance; it does not show crisis command or treaty implementation by Marcos himself."],"lenses":["1B.2","1B.4","1B.8","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"High Level Meeting to Commemorate and Promote the International Day for the Total Elimination of Nuclear Weapons | Philippines","publisher":"Permanent Mission of the Republic of the Philippines to the United Nations","publication_date":"2022-09-26","url":"https://www.un.int/philippines/statements_speeches/high-level-meeting-commemorate-and-promote-international-day-total-elimination","claim":"The Philippine delegation said President Marcos had told the General Assembly to reject deterrence and stay committed to decreasing the global nuclear stockpile, while warning that nuclear weapons plus emerging technologies could bring humanity closer to extinction.","locator":"lines 41-47","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Dated UN statement explicitly attributes the earlier presidential remark.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"The statement explicitly attributes the deterrence line to President Marcos.","contrary_evidence":["This is a public normative statement, not evidence of command-and-control or a concrete nuclear-security reform."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.2","1B.4","1B.8","1B.9","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Thematic Debate Cluster 1: Nuclear Weapons | Philippines","publisher":"Permanent Mission of the Republic of the Philippines to the United Nations","publication_date":"2022-10-14","url":"https://www.un.int/philippines/statements_speeches/thematic-debate-cluster-1-nuclear-weapons","claim":"The delegation said the Philippines' position is anchored in constitutional renunciation and a Southeast Asia nuclear-weapon-free zone, and that it had been reaffirmed by President Marcos.","locator":"lines 43-45","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official UN mission statement, dated and explicitly presidentially attributed.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Marcos administration's public position, not to nuclear-force management.","contrary_evidence":["Again, this is stance-setting, not an operational nuclear-risk intervention."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.2","1B.4","1B.8","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Ferdinand Marcos Jr.'s State of the Nation Address, July 25, 2022","publisher":"GMA News Online","publication_date":"2022-07-25","url":"https://www.gmanetwork.com/news/topstories/nation/839370/ferdinand-marcos-jr-s-state-of-the-nation-address-july-25-2022/story/","claim":"Marcos said it was time to re-examine the Philippines' strategy toward building nuclear power plants and that any program would comply with IAEA regulations.","locator":"lines 430-432","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct transcript of the presidential address from a major independent outlet.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and ruler-authored.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Marcos statement, but about civilian energy policy rather than nuclear weapons.","contrary_evidence":["This is civilian nuclear-energy exploration, not evidence of nuclear-weapon risk or reckless escalation."],"lenses":["1B.7","1B.9","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Philippines eyes investments on modular nuclear plants from US companies","publisher":"GMA News Online","publication_date":"2022-09-23","url":"https://www.gmanetwork.com/news/money/economy/845768/philippines-eyes-investments-on-modular-nuclear-plants-from-us-companies/story/","claim":"The Philippines was looking at investments in modular nuclear plants, and officials said Marcos had met with companies that could supply them and that regulatory work was being accelerated.","locator":"lines 114-130","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent media report with named officials and direct quotes.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Marcos administration decisions and meetings; still civilian-energy policy, not weapons policy.","contrary_evidence":["The report shows interest in peaceful power generation, not any nuclear-weapon posture or unsafe nuclear use choice."],"lenses":["1B.7","1B.9","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"House creates special committee on nuclear energy","publisher":"Philippine News Agency","publication_date":"2022-08-10","url":"https://www.pna.gov.ph/articles/1180962","claim":"The House created a special committee on nuclear energy, and the article says the move was in line with President Marcos Jr.'s agenda of building new power plants and re-examining nuclear power strategy.","locator":"lines 65-80","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Government wire with explicit dated linkage to Marcos's agenda.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Marcos administration's policy environment, not to weaponization or crisis conduct.","contrary_evidence":["The committee concerns civilian energy infrastructure and does not show nuclear-risk reduction or deterioration by itself."],"lenses":["1B.7","1B.9","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The Philippines ratifies the Treaty on the Prohibition of Nuclear Weapons (TPNW)","publisher":"United Nations Treaty Collection","publication_date":"2021-02-18","url":"https://treaties.un.org/Pages/showActionDetails.aspx?clang=_en&objid=08000002805a27ef","claim":"UN treaty records show the Philippines ratified the TPNW in February 2021, establishing the baseline that Marcos inherited in 2022.","locator":"Action details rows 10-23","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official UN treaty registry with ratification metadata.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Inherited baseline entering 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Pre-Marcos state action; included only for baseline.","contrary_evidence":["This duplicates the UNODA ratification record and adds no Marcos-specific conduct."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.10"]}

Search and rejection summary:
- I screened roughly 30 distinct documents across UN mission pages, treaty texts, IAEA records, GMA, PNA, Reuters syndications, official-government pages, and later reference pages.
- Blocked pages removed: the official SONA pages at `stateofthenation.gov.ph` and `pco.gov.ph` returned 403/429, so I used the accessible GMA transcript instead.
- Duplicate removals: the UNTC TPNW action page duplicates the UNODA ratification page; ICAN status pages duplicate the treaty-status baseline; several SONA syndications repeated the same presidential text.
- Out-of-period removals: 2023-2026 nuclear-energy and defense items, plus later 2024-2026 missive-style coverage, were dropped.
- Weak/irrelevant removals: South China Sea, general inflation, and general governance pieces were rejected unless they contained a direct nuclear or existential-risk choice.
- Overlap removals: some Reuters/Reuters-syndicated articles on nuclear energy were useful only as background, but the GMA/UN/PNA sources above were more direct and less duplicated.

Remaining gaps:
- There is no evidence of a Marcos-controlled nuclear arsenal, command-and-control system, or nuclear crisis, so 1B.3, 1B.5, 1B.6, and 1B.8 remain structurally thin by design.
- I found no 2022 ruler-specific decisions on cyber, biological, or AI catastrophic-risk governance that would materially bear on 1B.9 beyond the Philippine delegation's general warning about emerging technologies.
- More searching would only help if you want deeper treatment of civilian nuclear-energy regulation or cabinet-level records; it is unlikely to change the core conclusion that this is a limited-exposure, non-scored chapter for 2022.
